  "sourceNotes": {
    "raw": "The particle 咗 (zo2) marks completed actions, similar to past tense. It appears after verbs: 講咗 means 'said/has said'. The question word 乜嘢 (mat1 ye5) means 'what' and appears in different positions than English - note how 講咗乜嘢 puts the question word after the verb.\n\nThe time word 啱啱 (ngaam1 ngaam1) means 'just now' and appears before the subject or verb. This placement is crucial - saying 陳生啱啱講 sounds more natural than other orders. The classifier 啲 (di1) after 乜嘢 adds a plural or indefinite sense: 'some things' or 'what things'.\n\n佢話 (keoi5 wa6) is the standard way to report what someone said, literally 'he/she says'. Unlike English indirect speech, Cantonese keeps the original tense: 佢話聽日 directly quotes without changing time references. The question 幾點鐘 (gei2 dim2 zung1) asks 'what time' and always includes 鐘 for clock time.\n\nPronunciation tip: 咗 (zo2) has a mid-rising tone that many learners flatten. Practice the rise: zo↗. Common mistake: using 咩 (me1) instead of 乜嘢 in this context - 咩 works better at sentence end for confirmation questions."
  }
